course: religion-ethics
lesson: 88
title: Vows, oaths and promises in faith
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A vow is a solemn promise, often made before God and other people, and an oath is a promise to tell the truth or keep your word, sometimes "sworn" on a holy book.
explain: At a baptism the parents and godparents promise, in front of the whole church, to bring the baby up in the faith; saying it in public means the whole community helps them keep it.
explain: Courts make witnesses swear "the truth, the whole truth and nothing but the truth" because someone's freedom depends on it; in UK courts you may swear on a holy book or make a non-religious promise called an affirmation.
explain: Jesus said, "All you need to say is simply 'Yes' or 'No'" (Matthew 5:34-37), so Quakers never swear oaths: an honest person's plain word should be as good as an oath.
explain: Many faiths treat a promise as sacred, which is exactly why it matters to think before you promise, and to keep long promises one day at a time.
fact: At baptism parents and godparents make promises before God and the church; "When you make a vow to God, do not delay to fulfil it" (Ecclesiastes 5:4).
fact: Many cultures mark big promises with a ceremony so the whole community remembers and helps.
fact: The commandment "You shall not bear false witness against your neighbour" (Exodus 20:16) is first of all about witnesses; in Israel one witness was not enough to convict anyone (Deuteronomy 19:15).
fact: In UK courts people may swear on a holy book or make a non-religious affirmation; lying in court is perjury, a crime; witnesses promise «the whole truth» because leaving out important parts can mislead too.
fact: Jesus said, "Do not swear an oath at all... All you need to say is simply 'Yes' or 'No'" (Matthew 5:34-37); Quakers take this literally.
fact: The law in many countries lets people affirm instead of swearing, partly because of Quakers.
fact: James says, "Above all, my brothers and sisters, do not swear... All you need to say is a simple 'Yes' or 'No'" (James 5:12); a plain yes, kept, beats any dramatic oath.
fact: Scouting's founder Robert Baden-Powell made the promise central; the first Scout Law (1908) said, "A Scout's honour is to be trusted." Brownies promise to do their best and help other people.
fact: Joshua said, "As for me and my household, we will serve the Lord" (Joshua 24:15): a public commitment.
fact: Christians see wedding vows, "for better, for worse", as a lifelong promise made with God's help; researchers say honesty and forgiveness matter most in long marriages.
fact: In the Ramayana Rama goes into exile (sent away to live far from home) to keep his father's word; the Qur'an says, "Fulfil every commitment" (Qur'an 17:34); Psalm 15:4-5 praises one who "keeps an oath even when it hurts".
fact: Israel's leaders were tricked by the Gibeonites into a treaty but kept it: "We have given them our oath by the Lord... we cannot touch them now" (Joshua 9:19); today most laws say a promise won by trickery does not bind.
fact: To get out of a promise honestly, ask early and tell the truth; "Do not lie to each other" (Colossians 3:9).
fact: In Hindi and some other Indian languages «beta» is a loving word for a child.
fact: Small kept routines, like checking under a little sister's bed for monsters, help little children feel safe; "keep faith with the little ones" (see Mark 9:42).
exercise: What do parents and godparents promise at a baptism? | To bring the baby up in the faith | To pay for the baby's school uniform and books | To take the baby to church every single day | To buy the baby its own holy book to keep
exercise: Why are vows often made in public? | So the whole community remembers and helps you keep them | So that people can laugh if you break them | Because private promises are against the law | Because vows only count if a crowd claps
exercise: Why do courts make witnesses swear to tell the truth? | Because someone's freedom depends on what they say | Because judges enjoy hearing long and solemn promises | Because most witnesses tell lies unless they are watched | Because it makes the trial last longer
exercise: What is lying in court called? | Perjury | Parody | Penalty | Pardon
exercise: What is an affirmation in a UK court? | A non-religious promise to tell the truth | A short prayer said by the judge before the trial | The holy book that witnesses hold for an oath | A fine paid by someone caught lying in court
exercise: Why do Quakers never swear oaths in court? | Jesus said to simply say "Yes" or "No", so they are truthful always | Quakers are not allowed to go to court, so they never need to swear | Quakers think courts are always wrong | They swear on a different holy book instead
exercise: Lily says, "Cross my heart and hope to die, I'll bring your book back." What does Mum mean by "Just say yes, love, and bring it"? | A plain yes, kept, beats any dramatic oath | Lily should cross her heart twice to be sure | Lily should never borrow books again | Promises about books do not matter
exercise: What does the Brownie promise include? | To do your best and to help other people | To win every badge in the first year | To obey every older child in the pack | To never tell anybody about Brownies
exercise: What did the first Scout Law (1908) say? | A Scout's honour is to be trusted | A Scout must always be first in every race | A Scout never gets lost, even in the dark | A Scout must never cry or show he is scared
exercise: How do people keep a promise for fifty years of marriage? | One day at a time, with honesty and lots of forgiveness | By never arguing about anything at all, not even once a year | By writing the promise again every year | By living in separate houses
exercise: Hari's grandad says, "In our family a promise is kept, beta." What does "beta" mean? | Child | Grandad | Promise | Friend
exercise: In the Ramayana, what did Rama do to keep his father's word? | He went into exile | He became king at once | He married Kaikeyi | He fought his father
exercise: What does the Qur'an say about commitments (Qur'an 17:34)? | Fulfil every commitment | Commitments are for kings only | Break commitments if they cost money | Never make commitments at all
exercise: The Gibeonites tricked Israel into a peace treaty. What did Israel's leaders do? | Kept their word because they had sworn by the Lord | Broke the treaty the very next morning and attacked them | Asked the Gibeonites to swear a new oath | Forgot all about it and moved away
exercise: Erin said "100%" she'd come to the party, then remembered the christening. What is honest? | Tell her early, say sorry, and offer something else | Fake an illness on the day of the party | Go to the party and skip the christening | Just don't turn up and say nothing
exercise: Tom promised to check under his little sister's bed "for ever". What is wise? | Keep it while it helps her; one day she'll let him off | Stop at once, because promises made to little children don't count | Stop at once, because silly promises don't count | Promise to check the wardrobe too, for ever
test: Your mate says, "Swearing on your mum's life makes it way more true." What does James 5:12 teach? | A simple "Yes" or "No" is enough; your plain word should be true | Swearing on someone's life is the best and strongest kind of promise | Only swear on your dad's life, not your mum's | Swearing makes any sentence true
test: Toby asks, "Why do they say 'the whole truth' as well as 'the truth'?" What is the best answer? | Leaving out important parts can mislead, so witnesses must tell all of it | Because judges like long sentences and the old words sound grander in court | Because one truth isn't enough for a court | Because "whole" is just a nicer word
test: Hannah's friend says, "Quakers don't swear oaths, so they can lie in court." What is wrong? | They make a solemn promise instead, and lying is still perjury | Nothing; Quakers can say anything they like in court because they never swore | Quakers don't go to court, so it doesn't matter | Only Quakers are allowed to lie in court
test: "Cross my heart, hope to die, stick a needle in my eye," says Lily, and then forgets the book. What does this show? | Dramatic oaths don't make you keep a promise; doing it does | The oath was too short to count, so she needed a longer and scarier one | She should have crossed her heart twice | Books are not worth promising about
test: Ellie asks, "Do godparents have to keep their promise for ever?" What fits best? | It's a serious lifelong promise, and the church community helps them keep it | No, it's only for the day of the baptism, and after that the parents take over | Only if the baby remembers it | No, godparents never really mean it
test: The vicar asks, "Should Israel have kept the Gibeonite treaty?" Which answer gives reasons? | It's an open question: trickery usually cancels a promise, yet keeping your word can still be noble | No, because the Bible says they were wrong | Yes, because any promise made by anyone must always be kept | It doesn't matter, it was a long time ago
test: Erin texts her friend, "So sorry, got a bad cold," on party day, but she's at the christening. What would have been better? | Telling her early and truthfully about the christening | A better excuse that nobody could check | Going to both and leaving early from each | Ignoring the friend's texts all day
test: A cartoon shows a Brownie who says the promise and then laughs, "It's just words." What is wrong? | A promise said together reminds you who you want to be; it's meant | Nothing; promises at clubs are only words, and nobody expects children to mean them | Brownies don't have any promise | She should have said it louder
test: Lucy asks, "How do Grandma and Grandad keep a promise for fifty years?" What would they likely say? | One day at a time, with honesty and lots of forgiveness | They never had a single argument in all those fifty years together | They wrote it down once on their wedding day and then forgot it | Promises keep themselves automatically once you are married
test: Hari's friend says, "Only Christians think promises are sacred." What is accurate? | Many faiths treat promises as sacred, including Hindus and Muslims | Only Christians care about keeping promises; other faiths do not mind | Hindus think promises don't count | No faith thinks promises matter
test: What would happen if witnesses in court didn't have to tell the truth? | Innocent people could be punished and guilty people set free | Trials would be quicker and fairer | Nothing; judges always know the truth anyway | Only the lawyers would be affected
exam: Which of the following most accurately characterises the purpose of the oath administered to witnesses in a court of law? | The securing of truthful testimony, the liberty of persons depending upon it | The prolongation of proceedings for ceremonial reasons | The exemption of witnesses from the consequences of any falsehood they utter | The demonstration of the witness's religious affiliation
exam: Identify the principal reason for which members of the Religious Society of Friends (Quakers) decline to swear oaths. | Their literal adherence to the teaching of Matthew 5:34-37 that a simple "Yes" or "No" suffices | Their conviction that courts possess no lawful authority | Their exemption from the obligation to tell the truth | Their preference for swearing upon an alternative text
exam: Select the statement consistent with the account recorded in Joshua 9. | The leaders of Israel, having been deceived into a treaty, nevertheless honoured it by reason of their oath | The leaders of Israel repudiated the treaty upon discovering the deception | The Gibeonites were deceived by the leaders of Israel | No oath was sworn in respect of the treaty
exam: Ascertain the most honest means of withdrawing from a promise that cannot be fulfilled. | An early and truthful request for release, accompanied by an apology | The fabrication of an illness on the appointed day | The silent failure to appear without explanation | The quiet transfer of the obligation to a third party who has not been told of it
